# 💌 For Misa

A small website invitation for a walk.

I made it as a small pet project to practice connecting a simple frontend built with HTML/CSS/JS to a FastAPI backend and sending data to Telegram.

## What it does

* Shows an invitation to go for a walk
* The "No" button runs away when you hover over it
* You can choose a date, time, and meeting place
* The selected data is sent to the backend
* The backend sends a notification to Telegram
* Works on mobile devices
* Generates small hearts in the background

## Tech Stack

**Frontend:**

* HTML
* CSS
* JavaScript

**Backend:**

* Python
* FastAPI
* Pydantic
* HTTPX

**Other:**

* Telegram Bot API
* Render
* Git / GitHub

## Running Locally

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git
cd REPOSITORY
```

Create a virtual environment:

```bash
python -m venv venv
```

Activate it.

Windows:

```bash
venv\Scripts\activate
```

Linux / macOS:

```bash
source venv/bin/activate
```

Install the dependencies:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Create a `.env` file:

```env
tg_key=your_telegram_bot_token
my_chat_id=your_chat_id
```

Start the server:

```bash
uvicorn main:app --reload
```

Then open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

## Project Structure

```text
for_misa/
├── static/
│   ├── pngs/
│   ├── script.js
│   └── style.css
├── .gitignore
├── .env
├── index.html
├── main.py
└── requirements.txt
```

The `.env` file is not stored in the repository because it contains the Telegram bot token.

## How It Works

The frontend sends the selected data to:

```text
POST /submit
```

FastAPI receives the data using a Pydantic model and then sends a message through the Telegram Bot API.

The frontend and backend are part of the same FastAPI application, so in production the request is simply:

```javascript
fetch("/submit", ...)
```

## Deployment

The project is deployed on Render.

Whenever I update the code, I can simply run:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Update project"
git push
```

Render automatically builds and deploys the new version after the push.

## Why I Made It

It started as a simple idea to make a funny invitation for a walk.

At the same time, it turned into a small project where I could practice FastAPI, JavaScript, working with APIs, Git, and deployment.
